1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,867
By the end of this video, you will know whether your quad skates need 627 or 608 bearings, how many to order, and whether steel or ceramic suits your skating.

2
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:17,425
This matters because one mistake catches quad skaters more than any other.

3
00:00:17,425 --> 00:00:25,087
They order a set of 608 bearings, because that is what every shop sells and every article talks about.

4
00:00:25,087 --> 00:00:29,594
The box arrives, and the bearings will not go onto the axle.

5
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:33,274
That costs a delivery and a fortnight of waiting.

6
00:00:33,274 --> 00:00:34,851
The reason is simple.

7
00:00:34,851 --> 00:00:37,856
Many quad setups run 627 axles, not 608.

8
00:00:37,856 --> 00:00:43,189
The two bearings look almost the same in a photo, but they do not swap.

9
00:00:43,889 --> 00:00:47,568
Almost all inline speed skates use the 608 bearing.

10
00:00:47,568 --> 00:00:54,061
Inline dominates the speed skating conversation, so 608 has become the default assumption.

11
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:56,874
Quad skating sits outside that default.

12
00:00:56,874 --> 00:01:05,098
A great many quad setups, including quad speed and quad long distance skates, are built around a 627 axle instead.

13
00:01:05,098 --> 00:01:13,106
That one difference means a large share of the bearings sold to speed skaters will simply not fit a quad skate.

14
00:01:13,106 --> 00:01:16,641
None of this is a problem once you know about it.

15
00:01:16,641 --> 00:01:21,330
It only becomes a problem when you assume a bearing is a bearing.

16
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:24,793
So check your axle size once, and write it down.

17
00:01:24,793 --> 00:01:28,112
Then you will never order the wrong set again.

18
00:01:28,811 --> 00:01:31,869
The one number that matters is the bore.

19
00:01:31,869 --> 00:01:38,136
That is the hole in the middle of the bearing, the part that slides onto the axle.

20
00:01:38,136 --> 00:01:39,741
On the left is a 627.

21
00:01:39,741 --> 00:01:46,619
It measures 7 millimetres bore, 22 millimetres across the outside, and 7 millimetres wide.

22
00:01:46,619 --> 00:01:48,989
On the right is the common 608.

23
00:01:48,989 --> 00:01:52,352
Its only difference is an 8 millimetre bore.

24
00:01:52,352 --> 00:01:55,332
That one millimetre is the whole story.

25
00:01:55,332 --> 00:01:58,924
A 627 will not slide onto an 8 millimetre axle.

26
00:01:58,924 --> 00:02:02,593
A 608 will rattle loosely on a 7 millimetre one.

27
00:02:02,593 --> 00:02:09,777
Because the outsides look so alike, you cannot reliably tell them apart by eye across a table.

28
00:02:09,777 --> 00:02:14,439
The name on the bearing, or the axle itself, is how you know.

29
00:02:15,139 --> 00:02:20,058
Checking takes about two minutes, and it saves you a wasted order.

30
00:02:20,058 --> 00:02:23,487
Any one of these methods gives you the answer.

31
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:26,543
Do two of them if you want to be certain.

32
00:02:26,543 --> 00:02:31,163
First, pull an old bearing out of a wheel and read the shield.

33
00:02:31,163 --> 00:02:35,784
The size is usually printed there, so you will see 627 or 608.

34
00:02:35,784 --> 00:02:37,648
Second, measure the axle.

35
00:02:37,648 --> 00:02:42,194
A vernier calliper is ideal, but a decent steel rule will do.

36
00:02:42,194 --> 00:02:43,983
7 millimetres means 627.

37
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:45,771
8 millimetres means 608.

38
00:02:45,771 --> 00:02:51,063
Third, look up your plate or frame specification from the manufacturer.

39
00:02:51,063 --> 00:02:53,522
Most list the axle size directly.

40
00:02:53,522 --> 00:02:57,249
And if you are still unsure, ask before you order.

41
00:02:57,249 --> 00:02:59,485
A quick message costs nothing.

42
00:03:00,185 --> 00:03:03,082
Here are two more things worth knowing.

43
00:03:03,082 --> 00:03:05,682
First, 627 is not only a quad size.

44
00:03:05,682 --> 00:03:09,545
Some inline frames are also built around a 627 axle.

45
00:03:09,545 --> 00:03:16,231
So if you skate inline and your bearings sit loose, or refuse to seat, run the same check.

46
00:03:16,231 --> 00:03:17,940
Do not just assume 608.

47
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:22,323
Second, never force a bearing onto an axle to test the fit.

48
00:03:22,323 --> 00:03:26,335
A bearing that fits will slide on with light pressure.

49
00:03:26,335 --> 00:03:29,084
If it does not, it is the wrong size.

50
00:03:29,084 --> 00:03:36,215
Forcing it damages both the bearing and the axle, and then you have two problems instead of one.

51
00:03:36,915 --> 00:03:41,030
Once you have the right size, the next question is how many.

52
00:03:41,030 --> 00:03:44,596
Quad skaters sometimes order half of what they need.

53
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:50,631
Every wheel takes two bearings, one on each side, so the wheel spins evenly on the axle.

54
00:03:50,631 --> 00:03:55,157
A quad skate has four wheels, so that is eight bearings per skate.

55
00:03:55,157 --> 00:03:56,529
You have two skates.

56
00:03:56,529 --> 00:04:00,027
So a pair of quads needs sixteen bearings in total.

57
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:03,387
Two per wheel, four wheels per skate, two skates.

58
00:04:03,387 --> 00:04:05,513
Sixteen is the number to order.

59
00:04:06,213 --> 00:04:07,908
Now, steel or ceramic.

60
00:04:07,908 --> 00:04:13,531
The steel choice in the LiveLica range is the Power Bearings 627 ABEC 11.

61
00:04:13,531 --> 00:04:18,384
It pairs an ABEC 11 rating with P6 precision for a smooth roll.

62
00:04:18,384 --> 00:04:26,164
A nylon cage holds each ball accurately in the raceway, and double lumen contact seals keep out dust.

63
00:04:26,164 --> 00:04:33,020
For most quad skaters, from club level to serious training, it is a very capable bearing.

64
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:40,030
The ceramic choice is the Swiss Ceramic 627, built for quad speed and long distance racing.

65
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:47,888
It uses silicon nitride hybrid ceramic balls, which the maker says are about half the weight of steel.

66
00:04:47,888 --> 00:04:53,434
The ceramic is also cryogenically treated for efficiency and durability.

67
00:04:54,134 --> 00:04:58,149
ABEC ratings cause a lot of confusion, so let us be clear.

68
00:04:58,149 --> 00:05:00,780
ABEC measures manufacturing tolerance.

69
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:05,280
A higher number means the bearing was made to tighter tolerances.

70
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:11,649
What it does not do is describe everything that decides how a bearing feels under your feet.

71
00:05:11,649 --> 00:05:13,033
Cage design matters.

72
00:05:13,033 --> 00:05:14,487
Seal quality matters.

73
00:05:14,487 --> 00:05:16,010
Ball material matters.

74
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:19,610
And how well you look after the bearing matters too.

75
00:05:19,610 --> 00:05:27,155
That is why a good bearing lists its precision, cage and seals, rather than leaning on the ABEC number alone.

76
00:05:27,155 --> 00:05:29,440
So treat ABEC as one useful clue.

77
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:31,378
It is not the whole verdict.

78
00:05:32,078 --> 00:05:35,875
Fitting is simple once you have the right size and number.

79
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:39,410
Seat two bearings into each wheel, one from each side.

80
00:05:39,410 --> 00:05:42,617
Put a spacer between them if your wheels use one.

81
00:05:42,617 --> 00:05:49,032
Slide the wheel onto the axle, and tighten until the wheel spins freely with no side to side play.

82
00:05:49,032 --> 00:05:52,043
Overtightening is the most common setup error.

83
00:05:52,043 --> 00:05:56,037
It will slow you down more than any rating will speed you up.

84
00:05:56,037 --> 00:05:57,411
Then keep them clean.

85
00:05:57,411 --> 00:06:01,928
Sealed bearings resist dust and water well, but no seal is permanent.

86
00:06:01,928 --> 00:06:04,939
Grit that gets past will grind away your roll.

87
00:06:04,939 --> 00:06:10,503
Clean them when they sound dry or feel slow, and re oil them with a light, clean oil.

88
00:06:10,503 --> 00:06:15,675
Ceramic handles heat and water better than steel, but it still needs clean oil.

89
00:06:15,675 --> 00:06:18,228
Ceramic does not mean maintenance free.

90
00:06:18,928 --> 00:06:20,988
So which one should you buy?

91
00:06:20,988 --> 00:06:31,141
If you skate quads regularly, train at club level, and want a precise, well sealed bearing that lasts, the steel 627 is the sensible pick.

92
00:06:31,141 --> 00:06:41,441
If you race quads, especially over long distance, and want the lightest, most efficient option on a 627 axle, the ceramic is built for that.

93
00:06:41,441 --> 00:06:43,133
Both fit the same axle.

94
00:06:43,133 --> 00:06:46,297
The choice is about how hard you push them.

95
00:06:46,297 --> 00:06:50,712
And if your skates turn out to be on 608, you are not stuck.

96
00:06:50,712 --> 00:06:53,655
The ceramic also comes in a 608 version.

97
00:06:53,655 --> 00:06:56,818
Whatever you choose, remember the one idea.

98
00:06:56,818 --> 00:07:01,012
Match the bearing to the axle, never the other way round.

99
00:07:01,012 --> 00:07:06,530
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, and the link is in the description.
